অনুশীলন: এই প্রোগ্রামটি কোন ধরনের পলিমরফিজম দেখায়, এবং এর আউটপুট কী?
class Calc {
    int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
    int add(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b + c; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calc c = new Calc();
        System.out.println(c.add(2, 3));
        System.out.println(c.add(2.5, 3.5));
        System.out.println(c.add(1, 2, 3));
    }
}
ধাপ ১: Calc-এ add নামের তিনটি method আছে, যাদের parameter তালিকা ভিন্ন; এটি **method overloading**।
ধাপ ২: compiler argument-এর টাইপ ও সংখ্যা দেখে সংস্করণ বেছে নেয়, তাই এটি **কম্পাইল-টাইম (static) পলিমরফিজম**।
ধাপ ৩: add(2, 3) (int, int) ব্যবহার করে এবং 5 ফেরত দেয়।
ধাপ ৪: add(2.5, 3.5) (double, double) ব্যবহার করে এবং 6.0 ফেরত দেয় (double ".0"-সহ প্রিন্ট হয়)।
ধাপ ৫: add(1, 2, 3) (int, int, int) ব্যবহার করে এবং 6 ফেরত দেয়।
উত্তর: কম্পাইল-টাইম পলিমরফিজম (overloading); আউটপুট তিন লাইনে **5, 6.0, 6**।

example.en:
Practice: What is the output of this Java program?
class P {
    static void hello() { System.out.println("P.hello"); }
    void hi() { System.out.println("P.hi"); }
}

class C extends P {
    static void hello() { System.out.println("C.hello"); }
    void hi() { System.out.println("C.hi"); }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new C();
        p.hello();
        p.hi();
        C.hello();
    }
}
Step 1: hello() is static in both P and C, so C.hello() **hides** P.hello(); hi() is an instance method in both, so C.hi() **overrides** P.hi().
Step 2: p.hello() is a static call resolved at compile time from the reference type P, so it prints "P.hello".
Step 3: p.hi() is resolved at run time from the actual object (a C), so it prints "C.hi".
Step 4: C.hello() calls C's static method directly and prints "C.hello".
Answer: **P.hello, C.hi, C.hello** on three lines. Hiding follows the reference type; overriding follows the object.

example.bn:
অনুশীলন: এই Java প্রোগ্রামের আউটপুট কী?
class P {
    static void hello() { System.out.println("P.hello"); }
    void hi() { System.out.println("P.hi"); }
}

class C extends P {
    static void hello() { System.out.println("C.hello"); }
    void hi() { System.out.println("C.hi"); }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        P p = new C();
        p.hello();
        p.hi();
        C.hello();
    }
}
ধাপ ১: hello() P ও C দুটোতেই static, তাই C.hello(), P.hello()-কে **hide** করে; hi() দুটোতেই instance method, তাই C.hi(), P.hi()-কে **override** করে।
ধাপ ২: p.hello() একটি static কল, যা compile-time-এ reference টাইপ P দেখে ঠিক হয়, তাই "P.hello" প্রিন্ট হয়।
ধাপ ৩: p.hi() রান-টাইমে আসল অবজেক্ট (একটি C) দেখে ঠিক হয়, তাই "C.hi" প্রিন্ট হয়।
ধাপ ৪: C.hello() সরাসরি C-এর static method কল করে এবং "C.hello" প্রিন্ট করে।
উত্তর: তিন লাইনে **P.hello, C.hi, C.hello**। Hiding reference টাইপ অনুসরণ করে; overriding অনুসরণ করে অবজেক্টকে।

answer.en:
## Design

The class BankAccount keeps three **private** data members and exposes only safe public methods. This is **encapsulation**: the balance can change only through deposit() and withdraw(), which check the amount first.

Member | Access | Purpose
holderName, accountNumber, balance | private | Account data, hidden from other classes
getHolderName(), getAccountNumber(), getBalance() | public | Read access (getters)
setHolderName(name) | public | Controlled update (setter with a check)
deposit(amount) | public | Adds money; rejects zero or negative amounts
withdraw(amount) | public | Takes money only if the balance is enough
display() | public | Prints the account details

## Java program

class BankAccount {
    private String holderName;
    private String accountNumber;
    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(String holderName, String accountNumber, double openingBalance) {
        this.holderName = holderName;
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
        this.balance = openingBalance > 0 ? openingBalance : 0;
    }

    public String getHolderName()    { return holderName; }
    public String getAccountNumber() { return accountNumber; }
    public double getBalance()       { return balance; }

    public void setHolderName(String name) {
        if (name != null && !name.isBlank()) holderName = name;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Deposit must be positive");
            return;
        }
        balance += amount;
        System.out.println("Deposited " + amount);
    }

    public boolean withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0 || amount > balance) {
            System.out.println("Insufficient balance or invalid amount");
            return false;
        }
        balance -= amount;
        System.out.println("Withdrawn " + amount);
        return true;
    }

    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Holder " + holderName + ", A/C " + accountNumber
                + ", balance " + balance);
    }
}

public class BankDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BankAccount acc = new BankAccount("Rahim Uddin", "0123456789", 5000);
        acc.deposit(2000);
        acc.withdraw(10000);
        acc.withdraw(1500);
        acc.display();
        System.out.println("Balance via getter = " + acc.getBalance());
    }
}

Output:

Deposited 2000.0
Insufficient balance or invalid amount
Withdrawn 1500.0
Holder Rahim Uddin, A/C 0123456789, balance 5500.0
Balance via getter = 5500.0

## Trace

Step 1: The account opens with 5000.
Step 2: deposit(2000) makes the balance 7000.
Step 3: withdraw(10000) is refused because 10000 > 7000; the balance stays 7000.
Step 4: withdraw(1500) succeeds: 7000 − 1500 = 5500.
Step 5: display() and getBalance() both show **5500.0**.

## How encapsulation is shown

• **Private data**: main() cannot write acc.balance = 1000000; the compiler stops it.
• **Validation in one place**: every change passes the checks inside deposit() and withdraw().
• **No setter for balance**: the balance can never be set directly, only moved by transactions.

Real-world example: in a bank's mobile app, the screen never edits the balance field. It sends a deposit or withdraw request, and the core banking system checks limits and the available balance before changing it.

Exam tip: mark the fields **private**, give **getters**, put the **balance check inside withdraw()**, and show a sample run with one refused withdrawal.

