Practice: Will this Java program compile? If not, what error appears and why?
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;
    public double getBalance() { return balance; }
}

public class Hack {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BankAccount acc = new BankAccount();
        acc.balance = 1000000;
        System.out.println(acc.getBalance());
    }
}
Step 1: balance is declared **private** inside BankAccount.
Step 2: A private member is visible only inside its own class, and Hack is a different class.
Step 3: The line acc.balance = 1000000; therefore breaks the access rule at compile time.
Answer: it does **not compile**. javac reports "Hack.java:9: error: balance has private access in BankAccount". This is encapsulation at work; the fix is to go through a public method such as deposit().

