Assumption: the printed code is garbled and no instruction is given. It is read as "find the errors and write the correct program": a class Test with a private int, a constructor with a default argument of 0, getOne() returning the value and getTwo() returning value + 10, called on an object made with Test(5).

## Errors in the printed code

• **Class test()**: the keyword is lower-case class, a class name takes no parentheses, and the name must match Test used later (C++ is case-sensitive).
• **Int / Public**: keywords are lower-case: int and public.
• **Constructor name t**: a constructor must have the same name as the class, Test.
• **Missing data member name**: "Int" alone declares nothing; it needs a name, e.g. int value;.
• **t.getone(value) inside the class**: member functions are declared with a return type and body, not called with an object name.
• **Missing semicolon after the class**: a class definition ends with "};".
• **Test (5); in main**: this makes a temporary object with no name; an object name is needed, e.g. Test t(5);.
• **getone in cout**: a member function must be called on an object with brackets, t.getOne().
• **Label**: the second line prints "Get One" again; it should say "Get Two".

Compiling the printed code (with its capital Class read as class) with a C++ compiler stops with 5 errors (expected unqualified-id after "class test", unknown type name 'Int', undeclared Test, getone and gettwo).

## Corrected program

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Test {
    int value;
public:
    Test(int v = 0) { value = v; }
    int getOne() { return value; }
    int getTwo() { return value + 10; }
};

int main() {
    Test t(5);
    cout << "Get One " << t.getOne() << endl;
    cout << "Get Two " << t.getTwo() << endl;
    return 0;
}

Output:

Get One 5
Get Two 15

## Trace

Step 1: Test t(5) calls the constructor with v = 5, so value = 5.
Step 2: t.getOne() returns 5.
Step 3: t.getTwo() returns 5 + 10 = 15.

Exam tip: for "find the error" code, list every error with its **fix**, then give the **corrected program and its output**.

