answer.en:
## Inner class types

An **inner class** is a class declared inside another class. How its object is created depends on its kind.

Kind | Declared as | Object created by
Inner (non-static member) class | class Inner inside Outer | outer.new Inner()
Static nested class | static class Nested inside Outer | new Outer.Nested()
Local class | inside a method | new Local() in that method
Anonymous class | new Interface() { ... } | at the point of declaration

## Key rule

A non-static inner class object is always tied to an **object of the outer class**. So you must first create an Outer object, then call **outerObject.new Inner()**. A static nested class needs no outer object.

## Program

public class Outer {
    private int x = 10;
    static int count = 5;

    class Inner {
        void show() { System.out.println("Inner sees x = " + x); }
    }

    static class Nested {
        void show() { System.out.println("Nested sees count = " + count); }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Outer outer = new Outer();
        Outer.Inner in = outer.new Inner();
        in.show();

        Outer.Inner in2 = new Outer().new Inner();
        in2.show();

        Outer.Nested n = new Outer.Nested();
        n.show();

        Runnable r = new Runnable() {
            public void run() { System.out.println("Anonymous inner class runs"); }
        };
        r.run();
    }
}

## Output

Inner sees x = 10
Inner sees x = 10
Nested sees count = 5
Anonymous inner class runs

• The inner class reads the **private** field x of Outer directly, because it lives inside the outer object.
• Inside main (a static method) writing new Inner() alone fails with "non-static variable this cannot be referenced from a static context".

Real-world example: in an Android or desktop banking app, a button's click handler is often an **anonymous inner class** that reads the private fields of the screen class it belongs to.

Exam tip: write the two syntaxes **outer.new Inner()** and **new Outer.Nested()** side by side; that difference is what the question checks.

