## Idea

A **factor** (divisor) of n is a number i with **n % i == 0**. Check every i from 1 to n. Read three numbers with **Scanner** and reuse one method for each, so the logic is written once.

## Program

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Factors {
    static void printFactors(int n) {
        System.out.print("Factors of " + n + ": ");
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter three positive integers: ");
        int a = sc.nextInt();
        int b = sc.nextInt();
        int c = sc.nextInt();
        printFactors(a);
        printFactors(b);
        printFactors(c);
        sc.close();
    }
}

## Sample run (input 12 15 7)

Enter three positive integers: 12 15 7
Factors of 12: 1 2 3 4 6 12
Factors of 15: 1 3 5 15
Factors of 7: 1 7

## Trace for 12

i | 12 % i | factor?
1 | 0 | yes
2 | 0 | yes
3 | 0 | yes
4 | 0 | yes
5 | 2 | no
6 | 0 | yes
7 to 11 | not 0 | no
12 | 0 | yes

Note: the loop runs n times, so it is **O(n)**. A faster version checks i only up to √n and prints both i and n / i.

Exam tip: show the **Scanner import**, a **separate method** called three times, and a **sample input/output**; examiners give marks for all three.

