Practice: Read three numbers and, for each, print the sum of its proper factors (factors less than the number) and whether it is a perfect number. Input: 6 28 12.

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Perfect {
    static int sumOfProperFactors(int n) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n / 2; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) sum += i;
        }
        return sum;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++) {
            int n = sc.nextInt();
            int s = sumOfProperFactors(n);
            System.out.println(n + " -> sum " + s + (s == n ? ", perfect" : ", not perfect"));
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}

Step 1: 6: proper factors 1, 2, 3, sum 6 = 6, perfect.
Step 2: 28: 1 + 2 + 4 + 7 + 14 = 28, perfect.
Step 3: 12: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 6 = 16 ≠ 12, not perfect.

Answer:
6 -> sum 6, perfect
28 -> sum 28, perfect
12 -> sum 16, not perfect

