Practice: Using an abstract class Shape with an abstract method area(), compute the areas of a 4 × 5 rectangle and a circle of radius 1, and their total.

abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();
    void describe() { System.out.println(getClass().getSimpleName() + " area = " + area()); }
}

class Rect extends Shape {
    private double w, h;
    Rect(double w, double h) { this.w = w; this.h = h; }
    double area() { return w * h; }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    private double r;
    Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
    double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
}

public class Shapes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape[] list = { new Rect(4, 5), new Circle(1) };
        double total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
            list[i].describe();
            total += list[i].area();
        }
        System.out.printf("Total = %.2f%n", total);
    }
}

Step 1: Rect: 4 × 5 = 20.0.
Step 2: Circle: π × 1 × 1 = 3.141592653589793.
Step 3: total = 20 + 3.14159… = 23.14 (two decimals).

Answer:
Rect area = 20.0
Circle area = 3.141592653589793
Total = 23.14

